A set X ⊆ R^n is said to be open in R^n if, for each point x ∈ X, there is some open ball centered at x that lies entirely within X. 
A point x ∈ R^n is said to be in a boundary of set X ⊆ R^n if every open ball centered at x, no matter how small, contains some points that are in X and also some points that are not in X. 
A set X ⊆ R^n is said to be closed in R^n if it contains all of its boundary points. 
Finally, a neighborhood of a point x ∈ X is an open set containing x and contained in X. 
